,"opponent":"LA","home":true,"pf":9.0,"pa":19.0,"off_snaps":63,"epa_per_play":-0.03265,"pass_rate":0.6825,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6226,"def_epa_per_play":0.1074},{"week":17,"opponent":"BUF","home":false,"pf":14.0,"pa":40.0,"off_snaps":59,"epa_per_play":-0.2091,"pass_rate":0.6441,"pass_rate_neutral":0.4737,"def_epa_per_play":0.1666},{"week":18,"opponent":"MIA","home":true,"pf":32.0,"pa":20.0,"off_snaps":63,"epa_per_play":0.06014,"pass_rate":0.6349,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6667,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1281}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2022,"team":"LAC","distance":0.4436,"shared":21,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"IND","distance":0.5728,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Shane Steichen"},{"season":2023,"team":"TB","distance":0.606,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"},{"season":2023,"team":"LAC","distance":0.6063,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Kellen Moore"},{"season":2024,"team":"NYG","distance":0.6063,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Brian Daboll"}],"defense":[{"season":2021,"team":"LAC","distance":0.6257,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2022,"team":"LAC","distance":0.651,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2021,"team":"CHI","distance":0.7065,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"PIT","distance":0.7165,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"CAR","distance":0.7204,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Ejiro Evero"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Jets went 5-12 in 2024 and were outscored 404 to 338. The offense ranked 17th in EPA per play and the defense 20th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. Their most distinctive trait was a pass rate that was among the highest in the league.","offense":"The Jets offense was about average by EPA per play. They threw more than almost anyone relative to the situation, even when the score did not call for it. Aaron Rodgers ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Garrett Wilson, who drew 26% of the targets.","defense":"The Jets defense was a little below average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was pressure rate, where it ranked 6th, and its weakest was takeaways per game, where it ranked 26th. Will McDonald led the team with 10.5 sacks, and Javon Kinlaw added 4.5, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Their scheme was fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature was a Cover 6 rate that ranked 5th highest in the league.","special":"The Jets were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 71% of field goals, which was one of the worst in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 19% of the money on the books. Their running backs were among the youngest in the league.","players":"Aaron Rodgers played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,897 yards. Garrett Wilson was the clear top target with 26% of the looks and 1,104 receiving yards. Breece Hall handled most of the work in the backfield with 62% of the carries. On defense, Will McDonald led the team with 10.5 sacks and Brandin Echols picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2022 Chargers and the 2025 Colts under Shane Steichen. Their defense looked most like the 2021 and 2022 Chargers, both under Brandon Staley.","trends":"The offense went from 32nd in EPA per play in 2023 to 17th in 2024, a big step forward. The defense moved from 3rd in EPA allowed per play in 2023 to 20th in 2024, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was early-down pass rate, which went from 47% to 53%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2024. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}